7. useLayoutEffect
useLayoutEffect is similar to useEffect, but it runs synchronously after React updates the DOM and before the browser paints.
Ex:
import { useLayoutEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";

function App() {
  const boxRef = useRef(null);
  const [width, setWidth] = useState(0);

  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    const width = boxRef.current.getBoundingClientRect().width;
    setWidth(width);
  }, []);

  return (
    <>
      <div ref={boxRef}>Hello</div>
      <p>Width: {width}</p>
    </>
  );
}
When?
Use it when you need to measure or synchronously modify the DOM before the user sees the result.
Examples:
Measuring element dimensions
Positioning tooltips
Preventing visual flickering
Interview answer:
"useLayoutEffect runs after DOM mutations but before the browser paints. It's useful when we need to measure or synchronously modify the DOM before the user sees the UI."

8. useDeferredValue
This is used when you have a value that changes frequently and the UI consuming that value is expensive.
Ex:
import { useDeferredValue, useState } from "react";

function App() {
  const [search, setSearch] = useState("");

  const deferredSearch = useDeferredValue(search);

  return (
    <>
      <input
        value={search}
        onChange={(e) => setSearch(e.target.value)}
      />

      <SearchResults search={deferredSearch} />
    </>
  );
}
Important
useDeferredValue doesn't debounce.
Debouncing:
wait 300ms

Deferred rendering:
React prioritizes urgent work
and delays lower-priority rendering
Interview answer: 
"useDeferredValue lets React defer updating a non-urgent part of the UI while keeping urgent updates, such as typing, responsive."

