26.Activity

<Activity> is a newer React API for controlling the rendering behavior of UI that is currently visible vs hidden.
Think about a tab interface.
Tabs
[Home] [Profile] [Settings]

You might have:
Home
Profile
Settings

When you switch from Home to Profile, you may want the previous UI to remain preserved rather than completely destroying its state.
Conceptually:
Activity
   │
   ├── visible
   │      ↓
   │   Active UI
   │
   └── hidden
          ↓
       UI preserved
       but work deprioritized

Example conceptually:
<Activity mode={isVisible ? "visible" : "hidden"}>
  <Profile />
</Activity>
When:
isVisible = true
the Activity is visible.
When:
isVisible = false
React can hide the content while preserving its state and reducing background work.
Why is this useful?
Imagine:

Search page
   ↓
User opens Settings
   ↓
Search page hidden

You may want:

Search input
   ↓
preserve typed value

rather than:

Search input
   ↓
destroy component
   ↓
return
   ↓
start from scratch

Activity provides a mechanism for managing this hidden UI.

Important mental model
Normal conditional rendering
{show && <Component />}
false
 ↓
Component removed
 ↓
State lost

Activity:

<Activity mode="hidden">
   <Component />
</Activity>

hidden
 ↓
UI hidden
 ↓
state can be preserved
 ↓
background work is deprioritized
Interview answer:
"Activity lets React manage UI that is currently visible versus hidden while preserving the hidden subtree's state and deprioritizing its background work. It's useful for interfaces such as tabs and navigation where you want to preserve UI state."


27.Context Performance Problems
Context itself isn't a state-management solution optimized for high-frequency updates. When the Provider value changes, its consumers can re-render. We can reduce unnecessary renders by splitting contexts, memoizing provider values, and keeping frequently changing state out of a broad Context.

28.| Context                          | Redux                                |
| -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| Built into React                 | External state-management library    |
| Simple global values             | Complex application state            |
| Minimal setup                    | More structured architecture         |
| Provider-based                   | Store + actions + reducers           |
| No built-in DevTools equivalent  | Excellent Redux DevTools             |
| Good for theme/auth/config       | Good for complex shared state        |
| Performance needs careful design | Fine-grained subscriptions/selectors |

29. Redux Fundamentals
1. Store

The store contains application state.

{
  user: {
    name: "Rasiga"
  },
  cart: {
    items: []
  }
}
2. Action

An action describes what happened.

{
  type: "cart/addItem",
  payload: {
    id: 1,
    name: "Laptop"
  }
}
3. Reducer

Reducer determines how state changes.

function cartReducer(state, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case "cart/addItem":
      return {
        ...state,
        items: [...state.items, action.payload]
      };

    default:
      return state;
  }
}
4. Dispatch

Component sends an action.

dispatch({
  type: "cart/addItem",
  payload: product
});
Reducers should be pure functions.

30.Redux Toolkit 
Redux Toolkit (RTK) is the recommended way to write Redux today.

Instead of manually writing lots of Redux boilerplate:
Action types
Action creators
Reducers
Immutable updates
Store configuration
Middleware

RTK provides APIs to simplify this.
Main APIs to know:
configureStore
createSlice
createAsyncThunk
createSelector

createSlice

Instead of manually creating action types and reducers:

const cartSlice = createSlice({
  name: "cart",

  initialState: {
    items: []
  },

  reducers: {
    addItem: (state, action) => {
      state.items.push(action.payload);
    },

    removeItem: (state, action) => {
      state.items = state.items.filter(
        item => item.id !== action.payload
      );
    }
  }
});
Store
import { configureStore } from "@reduxjs/toolkit";
import cartReducer from "./cartSlice";

const store = configureStore({
  reducer: {
    cart: cartReducer
  }
});

export default store;

Then:

<Provider store={store}>
  <App />
</Provider>
import { useDispatch, useSelector } from "react-redux";

function Cart() {

  const items = useSelector(
    state => state.cart.items
  );

  const dispatch = useDispatch();

  const addItem = () => {
    dispatch({
      type: "cart/addItem",
      payload: {
        id: 1,
        name: "Laptop"
      }
    });
  };

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={addItem}>
        Add
      </button>

      {items.map(item => (
        <p key={item.id}>{item.name}</p>
      ))}
    </>
  );
}
Redux Toolkit is the recommended approach for Redux applications. It reduces boilerplate through APIs like configureStore and createSlice, provides good defaults, and uses Immer to simplify immutable state updates.

31.Zustand / Lightweight State Management 
Zustand is a lightweight state-management library.
The key difference from Context is that components can subscribe directly to the store.

Zustand example
import { create } from "zustand";

const useStore = create((set) => ({
  count: 0,

  increment: () =>
    set(state => ({
      count: state.count + 1
    }))
}));
function Counter() {

  const count = useStore(state => state.count);

  const increment = useStore(
    state => state.increment
  );

  return (
    <>
      <p>{count}</p>

      <button onClick={increment}>
        +
      </button>
    </>
  );
}

