33. Data Fetching (fetch)
Interview point: Don't make the useEffect callback itself async.

useEffect(async () => {
  ...
}, []);

Prefer:
useEffect(() => {
  async function fetchUsers() {
    const response = await fetch("/api/users");
    const data = await response.json();

    setUsers(data);
  }

  fetchUsers();
}, []);

34.Request Cancellation
Suppose the user navigates away while the API request is still running.
Component
   ↓
API request ────────────────→
   ↓
Component unmounts

The request may still continue.
We can cancel it when the component unmounts.
That's where AbortController comes in.

35.AbortController
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();

  async function fetchUsers() {
    try {
      const response = await fetch("/api/users", {
        signal: controller.signal
      });

      const data = await response.json();

      setUsers(data);
    } catch (error) {
      if (error.name === "AbortError") {
        return;
      }

      setError(error.message);
    }
  }

  fetchUsers();

  return () => {
    controller.abort();
  };
}, []);
AbortController allows us to cancel an ongoing fetch request. We pass its signal to fetch and call abort() in the effect cleanup function, which is useful when a component unmounts or when an old request is no longer relevant.

36.Race Conditions in useEffect
Imagine a search box:

User types: "react"
       ↓
Request A

User quickly types: "react hooks"
       ↓
Request B

Suppose:

Request A → 1000ms
Request B → 200ms

B finishes first:
B → react hooks

Then A finishes:
A → react

Now the UI might incorrectly show:
react
even though the latest search was:
react hooks
That's a race condition.

Solution: Abort old request
query = react
   ↓
Request A

query = react hooks
   ↓
Cleanup A → abort A
   ↓
Request B
race condition can happen when multiple asynchronous requests are triggered and an older request finishes after a newer request, overwriting the latest data. I can prevent this by cancelling the previous request with AbortController or by tracking request identity before updating state

36.Infinite Scrolling
the application loads more data when the user reaches the bottom.

Page 1
   ↓
User scrolls
   ↓
Page 2
   ↓
User scrolls
   ↓
Page 3

A common modern approach is IntersectionObserver.
useEffect(() => {
  const observer = new IntersectionObserver(entries => {
    if (entries[0].isIntersecting) {
      loadMore();
    }
  });

  if (loaderRef.current) {
    observer.observe(loaderRef.current);
  }

  return () => observer.disconnect();
}, []);

                User
                 ↓
            Scrolls down
                 ↓
        Bottom element visible
                 ↓
       IntersectionObserver
                 ↓
            loadMore()
                 ↓
            API page 2
                 ↓
          Append new data

